    "readTime": "3 min read",
    "image": "/blog/articles/xbox-game-pass.jpg",
    "imageAlt": "Pinterest photo of Xbox gaming lounge and wireless controller",
    "body": "\n      <p>Microsoft has revealed its second wave of late-November and early-December additions for Xbox Game Pass, with Red Barrels' intense survival horror title <em>The Outlast Trials</em> taking the spotlight. Announced on Xbox Wire by Megan Spurr on November 17, 2026, the rollout spans tactical stealth, early-access competitive action, whimsical adventure, and nerve-shredding cooperative horror.</p>\n\n      <p><em>The Outlast Trials</em> joins the library on December 1, 2026 across Cloud, Xbox Series X|S, and PC for Xbox Game Pass Ultimate, Xbox Game Pass Premium, and PC Game Pass members. Set during the Cold War, the game drops players into the Murkoff Corporation's disturbing mind-control experiments, playable solo or with friends in frantic four-player co-op where stealth and quick communication are the only way out alive.</p>\n\n      <h2>Every title confirmed in the Wave 2 schedule</h2>\n      <p>Before December arrives, subscribers have two late-November drops to keep their queues busy. On November 24, <em>Sniper Ghost Warrior Contracts 2</em> brings long-range tactical sniping and open-ended sandbox missions to the catalog. Three days later, on November 27, <em>Arkheron</em> enters the service in Game Preview, giving subscribers an early hands-on look while development continues.</p>\n\n      <p>December 1 delivers a sharp tonal contrast right alongside <em>The Outlast Trials</em> with the arrival of <em>Hela: of Mice &amp; Magic</em>. Together, the two December 1 additions give households both a lighter adventure option and a high-stress late-night co-op thriller just as winter break gets underway.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why the tier alignment matters</h2>\n      <p>Making <em>The Outlast Trials</em> available simultaneously across Ultimate, Premium, and PC Game Pass ensures multiplayer lobbies should see a healthy influx of new players right out of the gate on December 1. With Xbox's Black Friday Sale also opening on November 19, late November is shaping up to be an active stretch for anyone plugged into Microsoft's gaming ecosystem.</p>\n    "
