    "image": "/blog/articles/nvidia-q3-fy27-earnings-115-billion.jpg",
    "imageAlt": "Pinterest sleek aesthetic hardware photography of Nvidia flagship graphics architecture",
    "body": "<p>Nvidia reported $115.8 billion in revenue for its fiscal third quarter of 2027 on November 17, 2026, more than doubling sales from a year earlier and easily clearing the roughly $109 billion Wall Street consensus. Revenue climbed 103% year over year and 20% sequentially, while GAAP diluted earnings reached $2.70 per share and non-GAAP diluted earnings came in at $2.68. GAAP gross margin held at a formidable 74.8%, reflecting the pricing power the company still commands across accelerated computing.</p><h2>Data center concentration hits a new peak</h2><p>Data Center revenue reached $107.6 billion, up 110% from the prior year and accounting for roughly 93% of Nvidia's entire top line. Hyperscale cloud operators, frontier model labs, and sovereign computing initiatives continue to absorb rack-scale systems as fast as foundry and packaging partners can supply them. Management projected fiscal fourth-quarter revenue of approximately $130 billion, signaling that backlog visibility remains robust heading into early 2027.</p><p>That extreme concentration cuts two ways for institutional investors. On one hand, triple-digit growth at a $400 billion-plus annualized run rate is without modern precedent in hardware. On the other, any pause in hyperscaler capital budgets or slowdown in downstream software monetization now hits almost the entire revenue base at once.</p><h2>Life after Jensen Huang and rising policy risk</h2><p>The report marked Nvidia's first earnings release under interim CEO Colette Kress following Jensen Huang's November 12 departure and Harvey Jones's appointment as board chair. Kress, previously the company's longtime chief financial officer, offered steady operational execution, though shareholders remain focused on the board's permanent succession plan.</p><p>Geopolitical pressure also sits squarely on the horizon. With the U.S. Commerce Department's November interim final rule tightening licensing on AI chip shipments to Southeast Asia and Brazil and curbing remote cloud access for restricted entities, Nvidia created a National Security and Export Control Oversight Committee chaired by Suzanne Nora Johnson. For investors, the numbers prove AI hardware demand is intact, while governance and trade compliance now carry greater weight in the stock's multiple.</p>"
