    "dek": "Roughly 60 employees were cut and studio head Heather Hazen departed as Take-Two and 2K reshape the veteran strategy studio amid reported XCOM milestone struggles.",
    "author": "Samira Patel",
    "authorRole": "Senior Gaming Editor",
    "publishedAgo": "Nov 15, 2026",
    "publishedDate": "Nov 15, 2026",
    "readTime": "4 min read",
    "image": "/blog/articles/firaxis-layoffs-civilization-xcom-2026.jpg",
    "imageAlt": "Pinterest screenshot of turn based historical strategy game world map",
    "body": "<p>Strategy gaming suffered a heavy blow in November 2026 after Take-Two Interactive and 2K enacted steep layoffs at Firaxis Games, cutting roughly one third of the studio's workforce, or around 60 employees. Alongside the staff reductions, studio head Heather Hazen exited the Maryland developer, marking one of the most dramatic leadership and staffing shake-ups in Firaxis's thirty-year history.</p><p>Reports surrounding the restructuring point to mounting strain behind the scenes on an unannounced XCOM project that had struggled to clear internal milestone targets. Rather than continuing to fund ballooning production timelines across multiple large teams, 2K moved to shrink overhead and refocus the studio's immediate priorities.</p><h2>What this means for XCOM fans</h2><p>For players who have waited patiently since XCOM 2 and Chimera Squad for a full mainline follow-up, the news is deeply sobering. Firaxis had already navigated the departure of longtime XCOM creative director Jake Solomon years earlier, and reported milestone setbacks on the unannounced project leave the tactical sci-fi series' short-term future clouded in uncertainty.</p><p>Even if Take-Two preserves the XCOM brand on paper, losing roughly sixty developers across art, engineering, and design inevitably slows or reshapes any parallel production. Fans hoping for a sudden reveal anytime soon should temper expectations while the smaller studio stabilizes under new leadership.</p><h2>Civilization remains the anchor</h2><p>Historically, whenever Firaxis tightens its belt, Civilization becomes the bedrock that keeps the studio moving. As one of 2K's most durable PC and console franchises, ongoing Civilization support, expansions, and long-term franchise planning represent the safest commercial runway for a leaner headcount.</p><p>Still, the broader industry pattern is hard to ignore. Even inside a publisher preparing to reap historic revenues from Grand Theft Auto 6 this very week, veteran studios face unforgiving milestone scrutiny. Rebuilding morale and creative continuity after losing a third of the building will be Firaxis's toughest turn yet.</p>"
